course: biology
lesson: 54
title: Migration: the great journeys
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Migration is a long journey that animals make, often every year, between places that are good at different times: one for feeding, one for having babies, one for surviving winter.
explain: In September swallows line up on telephone wires like beads on a string; a few days later they are gone, flying about 10000 km to southern Africa, where there are still plenty of flying insects to catch.
explain: Animals find the way with a whole toolbox: the Sun by day, the stars by night, coastlines and rivers, smells, and the Earth's magnetism, and a young cuckoo flies to Africa alone without ever having met its parents.
explain: Cartoons show birds flying south with suitcases and a map, and the old story said swallows slept at the bottom of ponds; scientists put tiny rings and trackers on birds and found out where they really go.
explain: The journeys are dangerous: crocodiles wait in rivers for wildebeest, city lights confuse night-flying birds, and roads cut across crab paths, so people now build tunnels and switch off lights to help.
fact: Barn swallows from Britain and Europe fly about 10000 km to southern Africa for winter, because there are no flying insects for them in a European winter.
fact: Arctic terns fly from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year, often 70000 km or more, the longest migration of any animal, and they see more daylight than any other animal: two summers a year give long days and plenty of food.
fact: About 1.5 million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, move in a great loop between Tanzania and Kenya, following the rains and fresh grass; their calves can walk within minutes of birth, because they must keep up with a herd that is always moving.
fact: Salmon hatch in rivers, grow at sea, and return years later to the river where they hatched, finding it mainly by smell, which they learned when young and remember for years; Pacific salmon usually die after laying eggs.
fact: Millions of monarch butterflies fly up to about 4000 km to the same mountain forests in Mexico; the trip north takes several generations, so no single butterfly makes the whole round trip, and the route must be inborn.
fact: Humpback whales swim thousands of kilometres between cold feeding waters near the poles and warm seas where they have their calves, eating almost nothing while in warm water.
fact: Geese fly in a V shape; each bird gets a little lift from the air swirling off the wing tips of the bird in front, and they take turns at the front.
fact: European eels swim thousands of kilometres to the Sargasso Sea to lay eggs, and the babies drift back on ocean currents; nobody has ever seen them lay eggs in the wild.
fact: Migrating birds use the Sun, the stars, landmarks, smells and Earth's magnetism; a bird using the Sun has an inner clock that tells it where the Sun should be at each hour, and experiments in a planetarium showed birds steering by the stars around the North Star.
fact: Every year tens of millions of red crabs on Christmas Island walk from the forest to the sea to release their eggs, which hatch in the sea, timed with the rains and the Moon, and people close roads and build crab tunnels and bridges.
fact: Young cuckoos are raised by other birds and fly to Africa alone, so the route must be inborn; trackers showed cuckoos crossing the Sahara in one flight lasting days.
fact: Bright city lights can pull night-flying birds off course until they tire out or hit glass, and some cities dim their lights in migration season to save them.
fact: Dams on rivers, roads, new buildings and the loss of resting places along the way all make migration harder today.
exercise: Why do swallows leave Europe in autumn? | There are no flying insects for them to eat in winter | They are scared of the snow and the long dark nights | They need to grow a whole new set of feathers in Africa | Their nests fall apart in September
exercise: About how far do swallows from Britain fly to southern Africa? | About 10000 km | About 100 km only | About 1000 km or so | About 100000 km
exercise: Which animal makes the longest migration of all? | The arctic tern | The barn swallow | The monarch butterfly | The African wildebeest
exercise: Why do wildebeest walk in a great loop between Tanzania and Kenya? | They follow the rains and fresh grass | They are running away from the crocodiles | They are looking for snow to cool down | They follow the very same old lion every year
exercise: How does a salmon find the river where it hatched? | Mainly by smell, remembering the water of its home river | By reading the stars through the water as it swims along at night | By following the boats upriver | It picks any river at random
exercise: Where do monarch butterflies from Canada and the USA spend the winter? | In mountain forests in Mexico | In deep, dark caves under Alaska | In the steamy Amazon rainforest | On small islands in the Pacific
exercise: Why does a humpback whale swim to warm seas? | To have its calf in warm, safe water | To find more krill there | To escape from the cold water that hurts its skin | To meet whales from other oceans for a race
exercise: Why do geese fly in a V shape? | Each bird gets lift from the wing tips in front, saving energy | So they can all see the leader's face and follow where it looks | To scare away eagles with the shape | Because the youngest must always fly at the very back, where it is safest
exercise: Where do European eels go to lay their eggs? | The Sargasso Sea in the Atlantic | The source of their own home river | A deep lake high in the Alps | The North Pole
exercise: Which of these do migrating birds use to find their way? | The Sun, the stars, landmarks, smells and Earth's magnetism | Only the Moon | Only the smell of the sea | Only the songs of other birds that have made the trip before them
exercise: Why do red crabs on Christmas Island walk to the sea? | Their eggs hatch in the sea, so they must reach the shore | To escape a forest fire | To find cooler sand to sleep in during the hot dry months | To eat seaweed for the winter
exercise: How does a young cuckoo know the way to Africa? | The route is inborn, as it flies alone without its parents | Its real parents fly next to it all the way and show it | Its foster parents show it | It follows the swallows
exercise: Why do bright city lights at night harm migrating birds? | They pull night-flying birds off course until they tire or hit glass | They make the birds too hot | The birds stop to eat the light bulbs | The light makes the birds fall asleep in the air and drop out of the sky
exercise: How do some cities help migrating birds in spring and autumn? | They dim or switch off building lights | They put bird food out on every rooftop | They paint all their windows black at night | They play loud music to guide them home
exercise: How did people find out swallows do not sleep at the bottom of ponds? | By putting rings on birds and finding them in Africa | By draining every single pond in England one winter | By asking sailors in Africa | By watching swallows dive into ponds
exercise: Why can a wildebeest calf walk within minutes of being born? | It must keep up with a herd that is always moving | Its mother cannot make any milk until it gets up and walks | It is born old | It is scared of its own mother
test: in the cartoon the birds pack suitcases and read a map going south lol. how do they really know the way | Sun, stars, landmarks, smells and magnetism, and some of it is inborn | A leader bird has been to a special school for it and teaches the rest | They just follow the nearest motorway | They fly anywhere until it feels warm
test: Grandad says swallows hide in the mud at the bottom of ponds over winter. Right? | No, that old myth was cleared up by ringing: they fly to Africa | Yes, they breathe through their feathers in the mud until spring comes | Yes, but only the young ones | No, they sleep in barns all winter
test: Why would a bird want two summers a year instead of one? | Summer gives long days and plenty of food, so it can feed all year | It hates the colour of snow | Summer air is lighter to fly in, so the journey is easier both ways | It does not, terns are just lost
test: An arctic tern that lives 30 years flies about 70000 km a year. About how far is that in its life? | About 2 million km | About 70000 km | About 30000 km | About 2000 km
test: How could a fish remember the smell of one river after years in the ocean? | It learned the smell when young, and its sense of smell is very good | It reads the river's name on the signs by the bridges as it swims up | It follows other fish that have a map | It never left the river at all
test: A monarch flies to a Mexican forest where it has never been. How can that be? | The route is passed on by inheritance, not taught | Its grandmother told it the way before she died | It follows the smell of chocolate | It is the same butterfly that came last year
test: oi why's that goose at the back of the V and not leading? | They take turns at the front because it's the hardest place | Back geese are the babies who are being punished for being slow | The leader is always the oldest and never changes | It's lost and following anyone
test: Why might it be so hard to watch an eel lay eggs in the middle of the ocean? | The sea is huge and deep, and eels are hard to follow there | Eels only lay eggs on land | Eels hide their eggs inside clams and close them up again | It is against the law to look
test: How could a bird use the Sun as a compass when the Sun moves across the sky? | It has an inner clock and knows where the Sun should be at each hour | It flies only at noon, when the Sun is at the very top of the sky | It follows its own shadow | It waits for the Sun to stop
test: Why would people build special bridges just for crabs? | So millions of crabs can reach the sea without being squashed on roads | To help the crabs go shopping | Because the crabs bite the tyres of the cars and the buses that pass | So tourists can get closer
test: What would happen to swallows if winter in Europe still had plenty of flying insects? | Some might stay instead of making the dangerous journey | They would turn into a different kind of bird | They would fly even further south than before, all the way to the ice | Nothing, they leave for fun
test: What else might make a long journey harder for migrating animals today? | Dams, roads, buildings and loss of resting places along the way | Too many maps | Better weather than before, so they get lazy and forget to set off | Fewer predators
exam: Identify the principal reason for which barn swallows depart Europe in autumn. | The scarcity of flying insects during the European winter | An aversion to the colour of snow | The necessity of moulting exclusively in Africa, where food is plentiful | The collapse of their nests in September
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the migration of the arctic tern? | It is the longest yearly migration of any animal, from pole to pole | It is confined to the coasts of Britain | It is undertaken once in a lifetime, at the age of about thirty years | It occurs entirely within the tropics
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the navigation of the salmon to its natal river. | It is accomplished principally by means of the sense of smell | It is accomplished by observation of the stars beneath the water | It is guided by the wakes of passing vessels | It is a matter of pure chance
exam: It is given that the young cuckoo undertakes its first migration unaccompanied. Ascertain the conclusion that follows. | Knowledge of the route must be inborn | The route is taught by the foster parents | The young cuckoo does not in fact migrate | The route is learned by following swallows
